{"name":"Chinese Lacquer","hex":"#60c7c2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lacquer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A glossy, resinous material used as a surface coating; either a natural exudation of certain trees, or a solution of nitrocellulose in alcohol, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply a lacquer to something or to give something a smooth, glossy finish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lacquer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Toxicodendron vernicifluum","blocks":[{"text":"Toxicodendron vernicifluum, also known by the common name Chinese lacquer tree, is an Asian tree species of genus Toxicodendron native to China and the Indian subcontinent, and cultivated in regions of China, Japan and Korea. Other common names include poison sumac, Japanese lacquer tree, Japanese sumac, and varnish tree. The trees are cultivated and tapped for their toxic sap, which is used as a highly durable lacquer to make Chinese, Japanese, and Korean lacquerware."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Toxicodendron_vernicifluum_01.jpg/330px-Toxicodendron_vernicifluum_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toxicodendron_vernicifluum","wikidata":"Q28933284"},{"title":"Lacquer","blocks":[{"text":"Lacquer is a type of hard and usually shiny coating or finish applied to materials such as wood or metal. It is most often made from tree sap and wax and has been in use since antiquity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/%E6%98%8E%E5%98%89%E9%9D%96_%E5%89%94%E7%B4%85%E5%A3%BD%E5%AD%97%E7%B4%8B%E5%85%AB%E8%A7%92%E6%BC%86%E7%9B%A4-Dish_with_character_for_longevity_%28shou%29_MET_DP704215.jpg/330px-%E6%98%8E%E5%98%89%E9%9D%96_%E5%89%94%E7%B4%85%E5%A3%BD%E5%AD%97%E7%B4%8B%E5%85%AB%E8%A7%92%E6%BC%86%E7%9B%A4-Dish_with_character_for_longevity_%28shou%29_MET_DP704215.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_camp